> Ownership, finite arithmetic and honest query cost

The index now copies complete finite rectangular input data. Destroying or
changing the caller’s vectors cannot invalidate it or silently mismatch old
buckets and new coordinates. Copies of the index own their data, planes and
buckets. Const queries share no mutable state. This intentionally fixes the
earlier borrowed-pointer lifetime/stale-bucket hazard. Nonempty input requires
positive dimension; query dimension must match exactly, including an empty query
for an empty-data index. Zero vectors are supported operationally with cosine0
and nonnegative-dot bits, but have no mathematical angle for the isotropic law.

Signature widths0..64 are checked; zero bits put every row in each table’s key0
and zero tables deliberately yield no candidates. A full64-bit key uses a safe
unsigned shift through bit63. Complete malformed/nonfinite inputs are rejected
before indexing, even if no tables would be searched. Finite ordinary dot/cosine
arithmetic retains its original order. If nonfinite raw arithmetic occurs,
positive max-magnitude scaling supplies the deciding plane sum or cosine; no
exact-sign certificate is implied by floating arithmetic. Extreme finite and
subnormal vectors are covered separately from ordinary seeded compatibility.

A query initializes N dense visited marks before searching, so total query work
is O(N+L*k*d+V+C*d), where V is actual bucket-member visits and C is distinct
cosine evaluations. This is not a worst-case sublinear implementation, even when
C is small. Index storage includes O(N*d+L*k*d+L*N) owned values/ids, excluding
map overhead. Trace storage additionally owns query coordinates, actual
projection records, table probes with their real members and candidate visits.
Logical admission counts each of those records before its copy, default one
million and zero disabling only the optional cap; it excludes fields,
capacities, model/runtime copies, visited marks and bytes.

The controls below show operational consequences directly. With zero bits, every
row is examined once despite duplicate visits across tables. With one plane, an
antipodal query to a single indexed direction produces no bucket candidate.
No-candidate results use SIZE_MAX and similarity sentinel−2, not an invented
nearest neighbor.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lsh_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| prescribed index size | actual dense seen entries initialized | actual bucket member " "visits | actual distinct cosine evaluations | actual winner |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}) {
        std::vector<std::vector<double>> data(n, {1, 0});
        dm::algorithms::CosineLSH index(data, 3, 0, 7);
        auto a = index.query_analysis({1, 0});
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.seen_entries,
                   a.bucket_visits, a.examined, a.best);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(a.seen_entries);
    }
    dm::algorithms::CosineLSH missed({{1, 0}}, 1, 1, 7);
    auto a = missed.query_analysis({-1, 0});
    std::print("\nActual antipodal one-plane result absent {}; examined {}; similarity sentinel {}.\n",
               a.best == SIZE_MAX, a.examined, a.similarity);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual seen entries", {37, 99, 235}, 2).title(
        "Actual linear visited-array initialization in every query").x_label(
        "indexed row count").y_label("actual dense marks initialized").save_svg(
        "build/lsh_work.svg");
}
```
